A thread that loses its units in a call gives the processor up at once, not at the end of its turn.
A revoke that unbinds its caller and stops for an interrupt would switch threads in the middle of the call,
which the host, making the call again at once, does not;
a call stopped for an interrupt is made again at once unless the tick ended the turn.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call stopped where its caller cannot make it again

